WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.360
So we made a video a bit ago where we VoD
Reviewed one of the users on Skill-capped.com,

00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:07.900
and talked about the different errors in the
way they peeked angles.

00:00:07.900 --> 00:00:12.269
This sort of brought up the point though,
that all different types of peeks in Valorant,

00:00:12.269 --> 00:00:15.769
are very situational.
The game is a lot more than just clicking

00:00:15.769 --> 00:00:19.610
heads, and there's a lot of psychology that
goes into why you would want to peek something

00:00:19.610 --> 00:00:23.440
one way vs another.
Today what we're going to do is go over every

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:27.580
single different type of peek that I could
think of, and talk about why, and where you

00:00:27.580 --> 00:00:31.890
would use one of these types of peeks.
We're going to start with the more common

00:00:31.890 --> 00:00:34.541
peeks, and go into the more advanced stuff,
so just stick with me.

00:00:34.541 --> 00:00:35.541
But lastly, let's move onto the last type
of peek that we're going to talk about, which

00:00:35.541 --> 00:00:36.541
is peeking through smokes.
Peeking through smokes can be really dangerous,

00:00:36.541 --> 00:00:37.541
but there are some general rules to follow
when doing it.

00:00:37.541 --> 00:00:38.541
Basically the first rule is that you should
almost never peek out of the front of a smoke.

00:00:38.541 --> 00:00:39.541
This is why having your smokes flushed with
the doorway is so important, because it gives

00:00:39.541 --> 00:00:40.541
your team the best odds to punish players
for pushing through them.

00:00:40.541 --> 00:00:41.541
Instead, when peeking through a smoke, you
want to peek through either the side of the

00:00:41.541 --> 00:00:42.541
smoke, or even better, the back of the smoke.
The goal here being to get out of the smoke,

00:00:42.541 --> 00:00:43.541
as soon as possible before your gun fight.
By strafing out of the smoke you hide your

00:00:43.541 --> 00:00:44.541
gun for as long as you can before taking your
duel.

00:00:44.541 --> 00:00:45.541
And by exiting the smoke from the back, you'll
actually use the smoke as cover so you can

00:00:45.541 --> 00:00:46.541
take a duel without being hindered by the
smoke at all.

00:00:46.541 --> 00:00:47.541
This rule should be applied, whenever you
exit from any smoke in the game.

00:00:47.541 --> 00:00:48.541
Something we need to cover next though is
probably one of the best types of peeks you

00:00:48.541 --> 00:00:49.541
can pull off, and that is a double peek.
Double peeking is quite simply, when you peek

00:00:49.541 --> 00:00:50.541
with a teammate.
Two of you peek the same angle, in an attempt

00:00:50.541 --> 00:00:51.541
to take a 2v1
This can be really great if you think someone

00:00:51.541 --> 00:00:52.541
is holding an angle, or in a specific position,
that way you and your teammate can double

00:00:52.541 --> 00:00:53.541
peek him, and at the very worst, trade out
the kill.

00:00:53.541 --> 00:00:54.541
To do this, generally what you'll do to avoid
catastrophe is have one player, generally

00:00:54.541 --> 00:00:55.541
the one in the back swing wide, and the other
one will swing close.

00:00:55.541 --> 00:00:56.541
This way you'll avoid getting collated, and
give your team the best odds of coming on

00:00:56.541 --> 00:00:57.541
top.
Normally what will happen is the player you're

00:00:57.541 --> 00:00:58.541
swinging will try to track the person who
is wide swinging, and it will result in them

00:00:58.541 --> 00:00:59.541
missing both targets, and lose the fight.
This tactic is incredibly common in higher

00:00:59.541 --> 00:01:00.541
level play, and is the easiest way to assure
your team success.

00:01:00.541 --> 00:01:01.541
Starting out with the Wide Swing.
This is probably the most common type of peek,

00:01:01.541 --> 00:01:02.541
because it's psychologically, it makes the
most sense.

00:01:02.541 --> 00:01:03.541
You want to swing around a corner and look
for a fight, so you swing wide, and scan for

00:01:03.541 --> 00:01:04.541
the enemy as you do this.
However, in many situations, wide swinging

00:01:04.541 --> 00:01:05.541
can actually be bad and here's why.
If you're wide swinging with 0 information

00:01:05.541 --> 00:01:06.541
of what is on the other side, you're often
times putting yourself in a very dangerous

00:01:06.541 --> 00:01:07.541
scenario.
This is because the enemies know where you

00:01:07.541 --> 00:01:08.541
can come from, but you have no idea where
the enemies are.

00:01:08.541 --> 00:01:10.700
Performing a wide swing with 0 information,
and using 0 utility, is what we call a "Dry

00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:15.630
Peek", and is generally a pretty bad idea.
You could be swinging into an Operator, maybe

00:01:15.630 --> 00:01:19.479
an enemy is playing an off angle that will
be difficult to flick to, maybe there are

00:01:19.479 --> 00:01:22.869
3 enemies on the other side, you don't know,
that's the problem.

00:01:22.869 --> 00:01:26.859
This is why, if you're wide swinging an angle,
it's important that you have some sort of

00:01:26.859 --> 00:01:31.169
idea of what to expect on the other side.
Other things to note with wide swings, is

00:01:31.169 --> 00:01:35.909
that the closer you are to a wall, the faster
your wide swing is generally going to be.

00:01:35.909 --> 00:01:40.030
This is why it's important to try and close
the gap against operator users, because it's

00:01:40.030 --> 00:01:42.939
going to be a lot more easier for them to
land this shot

00:01:42.939 --> 00:01:47.009
Versus Landing this shot
Moving onto something similar to a Wide Swing,

00:01:47.009 --> 00:01:51.329
and this is what people call a Ferrari Peek
I've heard this term used a lot, I'm not sure

00:01:51.329 --> 00:01:55.049
if this the common terminology, but this is
basically when someone takes a Wide Swing

00:01:55.049 --> 00:01:58.809
to the extreme.
They Wide Swing, and then keep running.

00:01:58.809 --> 00:02:02.459
This can be really strong because players
will expect you to stop, and will try to flick

00:02:02.459 --> 00:02:06.929
towards your head, but instead of stopping,
you will keep going, and make it very hard

00:02:06.929 --> 00:02:10.469
for them to land their shot.
This is especially strong when abusing run

00:02:10.469 --> 00:02:14.120
and gun on weapons like the Frenzy, the Spectre,
or the Judge.

00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:18.340
Even in close ranges you'll see players abuse
this with the phantom or Vandal, it's quite

00:02:18.340 --> 00:02:22.819
a laughable peek, but it's also something
that absolutely works, and you should include

00:02:22.819 --> 00:02:25.370
it in your arsenal.
Definitely not the most precise way to take

00:02:25.370 --> 00:02:29.360
down your opponents, but still good to use
at times.

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:33.100
Moving over towards the Short Peek though.
This peek is basically what you use when you

00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:37.680
refer to that slice the pie method a lot of
videos have mentioned.

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:41.370
You'll generally use this peek when you're
unsure of where the enemy is, but you aren't

00:02:41.370 --> 00:02:45.590
able to use utility to clear them out.
You'll slowly clear each individual off angle,

00:02:45.590 --> 00:02:48.440
until you're able to locate the enemy, and
land your shot.

00:02:48.440 --> 00:02:52.700
Generally, you'll want to perform this peek,
from as far away from the wall as possible,

00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:57.010
as we mentioned this is a slow clear.
If you try to perform this too close to the

00:02:57.010 --> 00:03:12.450
wall, you'll just end up getting yourself
killed due to 

00:03:12.450 --> 00:03:36.019
angle advantage.
Moving onto more of our Info related peeks,

00:03:36.019 --> 00:03:38.920
let's talk about Jiggle Peeking and Jump Peeking
next.

00:03:38.920 --> 00:03:43.360
Jiggle peeking often referred to as shoulder
peeking, is generally something you'll use

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:46.459
either to gain info, or to bait out a shot
from your enemies.

00:03:46.459 --> 00:03:48.740
Sometimes it can even just be used to get
the enemies attention.

00:03:48.740 --> 00:03:53.209
"HEY LOOK AT ME"
When doing jiggle peeking correctly, you shouldn't

00:03:53.209 --> 00:03:56.310
really be able to see anything on the other
side of the wall.

00:03:56.310 --> 00:03:59.859
This peek is most often performed with your
knife out, and standing close to the wall

00:03:59.859 --> 00:04:04.270
so you can peek as fast as possible.
You'll just barely show your shoulder, and

00:04:04.270 --> 00:04:07.349
often times this is enough to bait out an
operator shot.

00:04:07.349 --> 00:04:10.870
Once they've taken their shot, you can now
pick up the angle yourself, so that if they

00:04:10.870 --> 00:04:14.249
repeek, you'll have the advantage.
You can also use this opportunity to move

00:04:14.249 --> 00:04:19.000
to a location that isn't exactly on the angle,
to give yourself an even greater advantage.

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:23.270
When performing this peek vs someone who isn't
using an Operator though, it can also be useful

00:04:23.270 --> 00:04:27.340
to mess with their recoil, or crosshair placement.
Oftentimes players will try to spray at the

00:04:27.340 --> 00:04:31.009
corner, or prefire you when they see this
peek, and you can use this opportunity to

00:04:31.009 --> 00:04:33.979
combine it with a wide swing, and pick up
your well deserved kill.

00:04:33.979 --> 00:04:38.710
Now you might think, if I can't see a player,
what do I do if they don't shoot at me?

00:04:38.710 --> 00:04:43.110
Well in case you're specifically looking for
info, rather than going wider and getting

00:04:43.110 --> 00:04:47.560
yourself killed, a much safer peek you can
pull off is called a Jump peek.

00:04:47.560 --> 00:04:51.919
Jump peeking is great for gathering info because
it makes it a lot more difficult to headshot

00:04:51.919 --> 00:04:54.289
you.
When peeking wide, your still can run the

00:04:54.289 --> 00:04:58.240
risk of getting headshot, but with a jump
peek, it's very unlikely to get punished too

00:04:58.240 --> 00:05:02.110
hard for this when done right.
The most you'll normally expect is a body

00:05:02.110 --> 00:05:04.860
shot.
To perform a jump peek, approach the angle,

00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:09.160
and jump forward, and then immediately hold
the S and A key, or S and D key depending

00:05:09.160 --> 00:05:12.590
on where you're peeking from.
This is one way to perform it, the other way

00:05:12.590 --> 00:05:16.509
is to just swipe your mouse across the screen
to air strafe out of view, both of these work

00:05:16.509 --> 00:05:19.199
fine.
I personally feel the air strafe is better,

00:05:19.199 --> 00:05:27.919
but it's your call.
I generally will jump peek on agents who don't

00:05:27.919 --> 00:05:32.060
have info gathering abilities, and when I'm
not intending to take a fight.

00:05:32.060 --> 00:05:35.690
You're just looking for info with this peek.
For lower rank players, often times it's going

00:05:35.690 --> 00:05:39.760
to be difficult for you to know exactly when
to use this type of peek, but I recommend

00:05:39.760 --> 00:05:42.620
using it when the game is quiet...
too quiet...

00:05:42.620 --> 00:05:43.620
Now let's actually back track a bit. Remember
when we talked about Dry Peeking, and how

00:05:43.620 --> 00:05:44.620
it can be bad?
Dry peeking isn't always bad, in many situations

00:05:44.620 --> 00:05:45.620
it can actually be good.
Specifically, it can be good, if the enemies

00:05:45.620 --> 00:05:46.620
don't know your location, and you think you
can get the jump on them.

00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:47.620
Using utility would just announce your location,
and actually hurt your peek, instead you just

00:05:47.620 --> 00:05:48.620
don't use utility, and peek it knowing they
either won't be looking at you, or won't be

00:05:48.620 --> 00:05:49.620
expecting you to dry swing.
A good example of this is when playing Reyna,

00:05:49.620 --> 00:05:50.620
using your blind is just a huge announcement
to your location.

00:05:50.620 --> 00:05:51.620
Sure, it can provide a lot of value in the
right situations, but if I'm in a 1v1, and

00:05:51.620 --> 00:05:52.620
neither of us know where the other person
is, throwing out my blind is a really great

00:05:52.620 --> 00:05:53.620
way to tell them "Hey, i'm over here, come
kill me."

00:05:53.620 --> 00:05:54.620
Don't get me wrong, it can be great if they
end up stuck in a corner, and can't avoid

00:05:54.620 --> 00:05:55.620
the blind, but in most situations they're
probably going to just avoid the blind entirely,

00:05:55.620 --> 00:05:56.620
and then peek when it's over and kill you.
Moving onto Crouch Peeking, this is something

00:05:56.620 --> 00:05:57.620
you can do if you think someone is holding
your angle, and you want to throw off their

00:05:57.620 --> 00:05:58.620
crosshair placement.
Most players aim at head level, at least in

00:05:58.620 --> 00:05:59.620
the higher ranks, so by crouch peeking around
a corner, you can throw them off a bit.

00:05:59.620 --> 00:06:00.620
Now with this being said, it's not exactly
they type of peek that you think this is.

00:06:00.620 --> 00:06:03.600
You shouldn't be crouching when you peek the
angle, that's just a good way to get yourself

00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:06.110
killed.
Instead what you'll want to do, is crouch

00:06:06.110 --> 00:06:08.639
into the angle.
This way you can still get the full benefit

00:06:08.639 --> 00:06:12.889
of moving quickly, while also getting the
benefit of making it more difficult to headshot

00:06:12.889 --> 00:06:14.980
you.
This will take some practice, but once you

00:06:14.980 --> 00:06:24.639
get it down, it will look something like this.
As we mentioned earlier, this peek can be

00:06:24.639 --> 00:06:28.400
pretty good vs better players, but if you're
in the lower ranks, I don't recommend using

00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:32.050
this peek too often, as the players you're
playing against likely have worse crosshair

00:06:32.050 --> 00:06:34.490
placement, and you might just crouch into
you're death.

